-- Number types, used by the numeric-literal `Specify:` above and by the
-- operator-disambiguation and cast examples below.


[\natural]
Defines: n
Documented:
. called: "natural number"
Id: "1a102108-da2b-47b8-9435-8dce9a5d5f36"


[\real]
Defines: r
Documented:
. called: "real number"
Id: "a6deadf6-fcfa-4de9-bf31-d9fce2181151"


-- `Declares` introduces a command naming a *value*, so a use is written
-- `y := \the.command` rather than `y is \the.command`. A declared value must
-- state its type.
[\zero]
Declares: z is \natural
Documented:
. called: "zero"
Id: "d5c731fe-427f-4ccd-bb74-e2a576ac565d"


[\one]
Declares: u is \natural
Documented:
. called: "one"
Id: "8e3440d0-13a0-428a-98c2-252004727775"


[n \.natural.plus./ m]
Declares: s is \natural
when: n, m is \natural
Documented:
. written: "n? + m?"
Id: "f994c277-b741-484e-9024-b2818fae4a9b"


[r \.real.plus./ s]
Declares: t is \real
when: r, s is \real
Documented:
. written: "r? + s?"
Id: "b8441c4c-a230-4ac1-a540-3edebae751f8"


-- An `else:` branch is checked with nothing assumed about the operands, so its
-- target must be requirement-free — here, a command taking no arguments.
[\unspecified.sum]
Declares: g is \real
Documented:
. called: "unspecified sum"
Id: "969760a2-f402-4b97-b227-b578f7095d1e"


-- `Disambiguates` resolves one ambiguous operator into typed branches. Each
-- `when:`/`to:` pair is tried in order, and `else:` is the fallback.
[x_ + y_]
Disambiguates:
when: x_, y_ is \natural
to: x_ \.natural.plus./ y_
when: x_, y_ is \real
to: x_ \.real.plus./ y_
else: \unspecified.sum
Documented:
. called: "plus"
Id: "173a6ede-2b98-4da4-a5b0-40b44635add7"
